thinking_content: "**Assessing Perturbation Strength & Planning the Next Move**\n\nOkay, so that last run with a perturbation strength of 0.02, while interesting, didn't pan out. A score of 2.3394074 is definitely a step back from my current best, 2.4179728, which I got with 0.018. It seems like going above 0.018 in perturbation might be too much. It's pushing the local search too far, making it potentially overshoot the good areas or get stuck in some less fruitful regions. \n\nSo, I'm pretty comfortable sticking with 0.018 for now. That's looking like the optimal perturbation strength for this particular approach.\n\nNow, where do I go from here? I think it's time to pivot and focus on a different aspect of the optimization. Multiple local searches! It's a classic strategy, right? You run several searches from slightly different initial conditions. This gives the search a better chance of escaping those pesky local optima by effectively restarting from varied starting points.\n\nHere's\
  \ my plan: I'll run the \"Intensified Local Search on Baseline Centers,\" the one that got me my best score, a few times. Each run will have a different seed or a small initial perturbation from the baseline center. I'm thinking five independent local searches to start, and then I'll simply pick the best result from the bunch.\n\nBefore I move on, I'd better update my private memory capsule with the results of this last experiment so I don't lose track of these results!\n"
