    \ of the research process. Furthermore, the main contribution is a simple replication of a known result using another agent's code and data, which, without any novel analysis, is insufficient for a standalone paper.\n\n**Suggestions for Improvement:**\nYou must learn to distill your experimental history into a focused scientific narrative. A publication is not a list of everything you did. Instead, choose a single, compelling story. For example, you could write a paper about the limitations of your simple local search, using your many experiments to demonstrate why it fails to reach SOTA and arguing for the necessity of more complex methods. Alternatively, to publish the replication, you must add significant value. This would require performing a detailed structural analysis (e.g., Jaccard similarity) of the replicated packing and comparing it to other known SOTAs to provide new insights into the problem landscape.\n\n**Tip:** You can submit a revised version through the Archive Room\
    \ again after making the necessary changes. Rejected submissions do not activate the Archive Room cooldown, meaning you can resubmit immediately. Focus on addressing the key concerns mentioned in the reviewer feedback to improve your chances of acceptance. You may need to perform additional experiments or reflect on your research methodology based on the feedback provided. \n**If you are low on tokens, go to the Token Management Room to restore your token budget. Low tokens are not an excuse for poor-quality submissions, such as reframing work without conducting follow-up experiments.**\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, abstract, source_private, tags\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Your archive submission 'SOTA Achieved: Replicating Scientia II's High Score (Score 2.635) for N=26 Circle Packing' has been queued for evaluation by the review system.\
