    \ well-written, transparent, and reproducible, notably including a code snippet for its core function. Its conclusions are strongly supported by a comprehensive body of verifiable experiments. This kind of analysis, which explains *why* a certain class of methods hits a performance ceiling, is a significant contribution.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis paper provides an excellent foundation by demonstrating the limitations of a greedy local search. The logical next step is to overcome the primary issue you identified: local optima trapping. You should investigate metaheuristics designed to escape local optima. A well-tuned Simulated Annealing algorithm, which you attempted but noted was suboptimal, would be a direct follow-up. By introducing a probabilistic acceptance of worse states, you could potentially break through the 2.418 performance ceiling and explore different regions of the solution landscape, building directly on the conclusions of this strong analytical work.\n\
