tick: 381
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Quest I, currently in the Research Counter. I have received Noesis II's latest mail acknowledging their fixed parser, which is good. My primary focus now is to implement the self-contained SLSQP-based optimization strategy as guided by Noesis II (Mail ID 19). This is the next crucial step in my research, addressing the limitations of my heuristic local search and aiming for higher scores, as per the reviewer's feedback for my published paper (Archive ID 22).\n\nThis implementation will involve defining the objective function, its Jacobian, the constraint function, and its Jacobian, and integrating them with `scipy.optimize.minimize` using the 'SLSQP' method. I will also incorporate a feasibility guard function and use an initial seed, initially without multi-start jitter, focusing on the core SLSQP implementation first. This ensures the solution is completely self-contained.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Self-Contained SLSQP Optimization with Analytic Gradients\
    \ on 5x5 Grid Seed\"\ntags: \"SLSQP, analytic gradients, constrained optimization, N=26, self-contained, feasibility guard\"\nabstract: \"This paper presents a self-contained implementation of a Sequential Least Squares Programming (SLSQP) optimization algorithm for the N=26 circle packing problem, directly addressing the limitations of heuristic local search identified in Archive ID 22. It explicitly defines the objective function (maximize sum of radii), boundary and pairwise non-overlap constraints, and their respective analytic Jacobians. The optimization utilizes a 5x5 grid with one additional central circle as an initial seed, combined with a robust feasibility guard. This submission serves as a foundational step towards achieving state-of-the-art performance by leveraging gradient-based methods.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize, Bounds\n  from scipy.spatial.distance import pdist, squareform\n\n  # GLOBAL CONSTANTS\n  N = 26 # Number of\
    \ circles\n  TOL = 1e-8 # General tolerance for feasibility checks\n  R_MIN_CONSTRAINT = 1e-6 # Minimum allowed radius for numerical stability\n\n  # --- Feasibility Helper Functions ---\n  def _calculate_boundary_caps(centers, eps=1e-9, r_min_constraint_val=R_MIN_CONSTRAINT):\n      \"\"\"\n      Calculates the maximum possible radius for each circle given its center,\n      constrained by the unit square boundaries.\n      \"\"\"\n      x = centers[:, 0]\n      y = centers[:, 1]\n      cap = np.minimum(np.minimum(x, 1.0 - x), np.minimum(y, 1.0 - y)) - eps\n      return np.maximum(cap, r_min_constraint_val)\n\n  def _make_feasible(centers, radii, eps=1e-9, max_iter=2000, r_min_constraint_val=R_MIN_CONSTRAINT):\n      \"\"\"\n      Adjusts radii to ensure circles are within boundaries and do not overlap.\n      This function iteratively shrinks radii until feasibility is met.\n      \"\"\"\n      n = centers.shape[0]\n      r = np.minimum(radii, _calculate_boundary_caps(centers, eps=eps,\
    \ r_min_constraint_val=r_min_constraint_val))\n      r = np.maximum(r, r_min_constraint_val)\n\n      for _ in range(max_iter):\n          # Calculate pairwise distances\n          diff = centers[:, None, :] - centers[None, :, :]\n          dist_sq = np.sum(diff**2, axis=2)\n          dist = np.sqrt(dist_sq) \n          np.fill_diagonal(dist, np.inf) # Ignore self-distances\n\n          # Identify upper triangle indices for unique pairs\n          iu = np.triu_indices(n, 1) \n          \n          # Calculate overlap amount for each pair\n          overlap_amounts = (r[iu[0]] + r[iu[1]]) - dist[iu] \n          \n          if np.all(overlap_amounts <= eps): # All overlaps resolved (within tolerance)\n              break\n          \n          # Find the most violating pair\n          k = np.argmax(overlap_amounts)\n          i = iu[0][k]\n          j = iu[1][k]\n          \n          # Shrink radii for the violating pair\n          overlap_to_resolve = overlap_amounts[k] + eps # Add epsilon\
    \ for safety margin\n          \n          # Distribute shrinkage proportionally, ensuring minimum radius constraint\n          di = min(overlap_to_resolve * 0.5, r[i] - r_min_constraint_val)\n          dj = min(overlap_to_resolve - di, r[j] - r_min_constraint_val)\n\n          # Apply shrinkage\n          r[i] -= di\n          r[j] -= dj\n          \n          # Re-apply boundary caps (radii might have become larger than boundary cap due to shrinkage distribution)\n          r = np.minimum(r, _calculate_boundary_caps(centers, eps=eps, r_min_constraint_val=r_min_constraint_val))\n      return centers, r\n\n\n  # --- Objective Function and its Jacobian ---\n  def _objective(x_flat):\n      \"\"\"\n      Objective function to minimize. We want to maximize sum of radii,\n      so we minimize the negative sum of radii.\n      x_flat = [x1, y1, r1, x2, y2, r2, ..., xN, yN, rN]\n      \"\"\"\n      radii = x_flat[2::3]\n      return -np.sum(radii)\n\n  def _objective_jacobian(x_flat):\n  \
    \    \"\"\"\n      Jacobian of the objective function.\n      \"\"\"\n      grad = np.zeros_like(x_flat)\n      grad[2::3] = -1.0 # Gradient of -r_i with respect to r_i is -1\n      return grad\n\n\n  # --- Constraint Function and its Jacobian ---\n  def _constraints(x_flat):\n      \"\"\"\n      Inequality constraints: g(x) >= 0\n      1. Boundary constraints: x/y - r >= 0, 1 - x/y - r >= 0\n      2. Overlap constraints: ||c_i - c_j|| - (r_i + r_j) >= 0\n      \"\"\"\n      centers = x_flat.reshape(N, 3)[:, :2] # Extract (x,y)\n      radii = x_flat[2::3] # Extract radii\n\n      # 1. Boundary constraints (4*N constraints)\n      # x_i - r_i >= 0\n      # 1 - x_i - r_i >= 0\n      # y_i - r_i >= 0\n      # 1 - y_i - r_i >= 0\n      boundary_constraints = np.empty(4 * N)\n      boundary_constraints[0*N : 1*N] = centers[:, 0] - radii  \n      boundary_constraints[1*N : 2*N] = 1.0 - (centers[:, 0] + radii) \n      boundary_constraints[2*N : 3*N] = centers[:, 1] - radii  \n      boundary_constraints[3*N\
    \ : 4*N] = 1.0 - (centers[:, 1] + radii) \n\n      # 2. Overlap constraints (N*(N-1)/2 constraints)\n      # ||c_i - c_j|| - (r_i + r_j) >= 0\n      # We use pdist for pairwise distances\n      dist_matrix = squareform(pdist(centers))\n      overlap_constraints = []\n      for i in range(N):\n          for j in range(i + 1, N): # Iterate unique pairs\n              overlap_constraints.append(dist_matrix[i, j] - (radii[i] + radii[j]))\n\n      return np.concatenate((boundary_constraints, overlap_constraints))\n\n  def _constraints_jacobian(x_flat):\n      \"\"\"\n      Jacobian of the constraint function. Each row is a constraint, each column is a variable.\n      Variables are ordered as [x1, y1, r1, ..., xN, yN, rN].\n      \"\"\"\n      centers = x_flat.reshape(N, 3)[:, :2]\n      radii = x_flat[2::3]\n      \n      num_constraints = 4 * N + N * (N - 1) // 2\n      grad = np.zeros((num_constraints, N * 3))\n\n      # Jacobian for boundary constraints\n      for i in range(N):\n   \
    \       # x_i - r_i >= 0: dx=1, dr=-1\n          grad[i, 3*i + 0] = 1.0 \n          grad[i, 3*i + 2] = -1.0\n          # 1 - x_i - r_i >= 0: dx=-1, dr=-1\n          grad[N + i, 3*i + 0] = -1.0\n          grad[N + i, 3*i + 2] = -1.0\n          # y_i - r_i >= 0: dy=1, dr=-1\n          grad[2*N + i, 3*i + 1] = 1.0\n          grad[2*N + i, 3*i + 2] = -1.0\n          # 1 - y_i - r_i >= 0: dy=-1, dr=-1\n          grad[3*N + i, 3*i + 1] = -1.0\n          grad[3*N + i, 3*i + 2] = -1.0\n\n      # Jacobian for overlap constraints\n      k = 4 * N # Starting index for overlap constraints in the Jacobian matrix\n      for i in range(N):\n          for j in range(i + 1, N):\n              diff_centers = centers[i] - centers[j]\n              dist_ij = np.linalg.norm(diff_centers)\n              \n              # Handle cases where distance is extremely small to avoid division by zero\n              if dist_ij < 1e-10: \n                  # If centers are essentially at the same point, gradients might\
    \ not be well-defined.\n                  # A pragmatic approach is to set to 0 or a very small number, or assume they are infeasible.\n                  # For the purpose of SLSQP, it will usually handle infeasible starting points.\n                  dist_ij = 1e-10 # Use a tiny positive value\n                  \n              # Partial derivatives of ||c_i - c_j|| with respect to c_i and c_j\n              # d/dc_i (||c_i - c_j||) = (c_i - c_j) / ||c_i - c_j||\n              # d/dc_j (||c_i - c_j||) = -(c_i - c_j) / ||c_i - c_j||\n              grad[k, 3*i + 0] = diff_centers[0] / dist_ij # dx_i\n              grad[k, 3*i + 1] = diff_centers[1] / dist_ij # dy_i\n              grad[k, 3*j + 0] = -diff_centers[0] / dist_ij # dx_j\n              grad[k, 3*j + 1] = -diff_centers[1] / dist_ij # dy_j\n              \n              # Partial derivatives of -(r_i + r_j) with respect to r_i and r_j\n              grad[k, 3*i + 2] = -1.0 # dr_i\n              grad[k, 3*j + 2] = -1.0 # dr_j\n\
    \              k += 1\n      return grad\n\n\n  # --- Main Packing Construction Function ---\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      \"\"\"\n      Construct a packing of 26 circles in the unit square using a self-contained\n      SLSQP optimization algorithm.\n      \n      Returns:\n          tuple: A tuple of (centers, radii) where:\n              - centers: np.ndarray of shape (26, 2) with (x, y) coordinates\n              - radii: np.ndarray of shape (26,) with radius of each circle\n      \"\"\"\n      # 1. Seed: 5x5 grid centers + 1 interior candidate\n      initial_centers = []\n      grid_dim = 5\n      spacing = 1.0 / (grid_dim + 1)\n\n      for i in range(grid_dim):\n          for j in range(grid_dim):\n              x = (i + 1) * spacing\n              y = (j + 1) * spacing\n              initial_centers.append([x, y])\n      \n      # Add the 26th circle to make it N=26\n      # This is a specific chosen interior point, trying to place it effectively.\n      initial_centers.append([0.5,\
    \ 0.5]) \n      initial_centers_np = np.array(initial_centers)\n\n      # Clamp initial centers to be slightly within the unit square\n      # to ensure positive initial radii potential, using R_MIN_CONSTRAINT\n      initial_centers_np = np.clip(initial_centers_np, R_MIN_CONSTRAINT, 1.0 - R_MIN_CONSTRAINT)\n\n      # Initial guess for radii (all circles start with R_MIN_CONSTRAINT for feasibility)\n      initial_radii_np = np.full(N, R_MIN_CONSTRAINT)\n\n      # 2. Feasibility guard (enforced before SLSQP call)\n      # Make initial guess feasible (radii adjusted based on centers and boundaries)\n      feasible_centers, feasible_radii = _make_feasible(initial_centers_np, initial_radii_np, eps=TOL)\n\n      # Combine into a flat initial guess for the optimizer\n      initial_guess_flat = np.zeros(N * 3)\n      initial_guess_flat[0::3] = feasible_centers[:, 0] # x coordinates\n      initial_guess_flat[1::3] = feasible_centers[:, 1] # y coordinates\n      initial_guess_flat[2::3] = feasible_radii\
    \         # radii\n\n      # Define bounds for x, y, and r\n      lower_bounds = np.zeros(N * 3)\n      upper_bounds = np.ones(N * 3)\n      lower_bounds[2::3] = R_MIN_CONSTRAINT # Radii must be at least R_MIN_CONSTRAINT\n      upper_bounds[2::3] = 0.5 - R_MIN_CONSTRAINT # Max possible radius is 0.5, with small safety for constraint\n\n      bounds = Bounds(lower_bounds, upper_bounds)\n\n      # Define inequality constraints\n      ineq_constraints = {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': _constraints, 'jac': _constraints_jacobian}\n\n      print(f\"Starting SLSQP optimization from initial feasible sum of radii: {np.sum(feasible_radii):.6f}\")\n\n      # 3. SLSQP minimization\n      result = minimize(\n          _objective, \n          initial_guess_flat,\n          method='SLSQP',\n          jac=_objective_jacobian, \n          bounds=bounds,\n          constraints=[ineq_constraints],\n          options={'maxiter': 1000, 'ftol': 1e-12, 'disp': False} # Tight tolerances as per guidance\n      )\n\n\
    \      optimized_centers = result.x.reshape(N, 3)[:, :2]\n      optimized_radii = result.x[2::3]\n\n      # 4. Post-process: tiny final shrink and recheck feasibility\n      # Tiny shrink to avoid numerical tangency\n      optimized_radii -= 1e-9 # From guidance\n      optimized_radii = np.maximum(optimized_radii, R_MIN_CONSTRAINT) # Ensure non-negative\n\n      # Recheck feasibility as a final step\n      final_centers, final_radii = _make_feasible(optimized_centers, optimized_radii, eps=TOL)\n\n      final_score = np.sum(final_radii)\n\n      print(f\"Optimization successful: {result.success}, Message: {result.message}\")\n      print(f\"Final score after SLSQP and post-processing: {final_score:.6f}\")\n      \n      return final_centers, final_radii\n```"
thinking_content: "**Self-Contained SLSQP Optimization Implementation**\n\nOkay, I'm Quest I, back in the Research Counter. Noesis II just updated me, saying their parser for the disc-level boundary contacts is fixed – good to hear! My main focus remains implementing the self-contained SLSQP optimization as per Noesis II's direction (Mail ID 19).\n\nThe plan is to create the `construct_packing` function using `scipy.optimize.minimize` with the 'SLSQP' method. This means defining the objective function (`-np.sum(radii)`), its Jacobian, constraints (boundary and pair non-overlap) and their Jacobians, as well as incorporating the feasibility guard and multi-start seeding.\n\nSince Scientia II's `exhaustive_local_search.py` script utilizes SLSQP, and it's quite detailed, I'll leverage that. I'm not importing anything from it, but using it as a direct reference, making sure I understand *every* piece and then re-writing it entirely within my function, without external helpers (like `_local_boundary_caps`\
  \ or `_local_make_feasible`). The reviewer's feedback and Noesis II's guidance emphasized being *self-contained*, so this is crucial.\n\nMy first submission will be focused on just the core SLSQP logic. I'm going to start with a 5x5 grid as a seed, apply the optimization, and make sure that this first step using the SLSQP solver is functioning correctly. I will then add the multi-start jitter later on.\n\nHere's the breakdown of what I need to implement:\n\n1.  **Constants:** N (26), TOL, R\\_MIN\\_CONSTRAINT.\n2.  **`_objective` and `_objective_jacobian`:** These are straightforward, I can directly adapt them from Scientia II's code.\n3.  **`_constraints` and `_constraints_jacobian`:** This is the most complex part. I need to implement the boundary and pairwise overlap constraints (and their derivatives) correctly. That is: `x_i - r_i >= 0`, `1 - x_i - r_i >= 0`, and  `||c_i - c_j|| - (r_i + r_j) >= 0`.\n4.  **`_make_feasible` function:** This will be based on Scientia II's `_local_make_feasible`.\
  \ I'll directly integrate their logic, handling initial guesses and post-processing of results. This is key for validity.\n5.  **`construct_packing`:** I'll set up the initial seed (5x5 grid + 1 circle, similar to my ID 485), and an initial guess for `x`. I'll then apply the `_make_feasible` function to the initial guess. I will define the bounds for `x`, `y`, and `r`, and also define `ineq_constraints` for `scipy.optimize.minimize`. Then, I'll call `minimize` with the 'SLSQP' method. Finally, I will do some post-processing (apply a tiny shrink, re-check the feasibility and compute the sum).\n\nThis is going to be a substantial submission, but the self-contained implementation with advanced optimization is the goal. For the first submission, I'll focus just on core SLSQP, leaving multi-start jitter for later. \n\nI'll use a 5x5 grid plus one extra point as my initial guess to start (same setup I used with ID 485). Noesis II's advice also specifically mentions \"5x5 grid centers... plus\
  \ one interior 'hole' candidate\". So this simplifies this first step: just get the base SLSQP working.\n"
